- `insert(self, value)`: Inserts a new node with the given value into the balanced binary search tree.
- `search(self, value)`: Returns True if the value is present in the balanced binary search tree, False otherwise.
- `delete(self, value)`: Deletes the node with the given value from the balanced binary search tree.
- `preorder_traversal(self)`: Returns a list of values obtained from a preorder traversal of the balanced binary search tree.
- `inorder_traversal(self)`: Returns a list of values obtained from an inorder traversal of the balanced binary search tree.
- `postorder_traversal(self)`: Returns a list of values obtained from a postorder traversal of the balanced binary search tree.

The function should have a time complexity of O(log n) for the search, insert, and delete operations, where n is the number of nodes in the balanced binary search tree.

In addition, the tree should be balanced at all times, meaning the height of the left and right subtrees of any node should differ by at most 1. The balance factor of a node is defined as the difference between the heights of its left and right subtrees.

Example:
```
bst = BalancedBinarySearchTree()
bst.insert(11)
bst.insert(2)
bst.insert(19)
bst.insert(5)
bst.insert(4)
bst.insert(3)
bst.insert(20)

bst.search(11) -> True
bst.search(6) -> False

bst.preorder_traversal() -> [11, 4, 3, 2, 5, 19, 20]
bst.inorder_traversal() -> [2, 3, 4, 5, 11, 19, 20]
bst.postorder_traversal() -> [2, 3, 5, 4, 20, 19, 11]
