### Response:
In React.js, the "render" method is used to update the user interface by returning the elements that should be displayed on the screen. It is responsible for rendering the component hierarchy into the real DOM.

Here's an example of how the "render" method is used in a complex component hierarchy:

```javascript
class ParentComponent extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Hello, Parent Component!</h1>
        <ChildComponent />
      </div>
    );
  }
}

class ChildComponent extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h2>Hello, Child Component!</h2>
        <GrandchildComponent />
      </div>
    );
  }
}

class GrandchildComponent extends React.Component {
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <h3>Hello, Grandchild Component!</h3>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

ReactDOM.render(<ParentComponent />, document.getElementById("root"));
```

In this example, the "render" method is called on each component to create a virtual representation of the component hierarchy. The JSX syntax is used to define the structure and content of the elements to be rendered. The returned elements are then used to update the real DOM.

The virtual DOM in React is a lightweight copy of the real DOM. It is a representation of the current state of the user interface. When there are changes in the state or props of a component, the "render" method is called again to create a new virtual DOM. React then compares the new virtual DOM with the previous one to determine the minimal set of changes needed to update the real DOM.

The advantages of using the virtual DOM in React are:

1. Performance optimization: By comparing the virtual DOMs, React can minimize the number of updates to the real DOM. It batch updates and performs a diffing algorithm to identify and apply only the necessary changes. This approach is more efficient than directly manipulating the real DOM, which can be slower and cause unnecessary reflows and repaints.

2. Cross-platform compatibility: The virtual DOM provides a platform-independent representation of the UI, allowing React to work consistently across different browsers and environments. It abstracts away the differences in the real DOM implementations, ensuring a consistent behavior.

3. Easier development and maintenance: The virtual DOM simplifies the development process by providing a declarative programming model. Developers can focus on describing the desired UI state, and React takes care of updating the real DOM efficiently. Additionally, the virtual DOM makes it easier to reason about the state of the UI, aiding in debugging and maintaining complex component hierarchies.

In summary, the "render" method in React.js is used to update the user interface by returning the elements to be displayed. The virtual DOM, used by React, is a lightweight copy of the real DOM that enables efficient updates by comparing the previous and new states. The advantages of the virtual DOM include performance optimization, cross-platform compatibility, and simplified development and maintenance.

